The '''Constitution of the Empire of Mitoyagi''' (大美登八木帝国憲法, Dai-Mitoyagi Tei'koku Kenpō), commonly referred to as the Saito Constitution (明治憲法, Saito Kenpō), was the fundamental law of the [[Mitoyagi Empire|Empire of Mitoyagi]] (modern-day [[Okaiken]]). Proclaimed on January 3, 1871, it served as the legal framework for the nation’s rapid transition from a feudal society to a centralized industrial power. It remained in force until the Quiet Revolution of 1968 led to the proclamation of a republic in 1970. Enacted during the Saito Restoration, the document established a mixed system of constitutional and absolute monarchy, drawing heavy inspiration from the Brieslandicn model while maintaining the traditional Sanese concept of ''kokutai'' (national polity).

Under the provisions of the Saito Constitution, the Emperor of Mitoyagi was recognized as the head of state and the source of all sovereign power. While the document provided for a Prime Minister and a Cabinet of Ministers to advise the monarch, these officials were responsible directly to the Emperor rather than to the elected legislature. This legal structure allowed the early Meiji-era oligarchs to govern with a high degree of autonomy, navigating the complexities of modernization and foreign diplomacy during the late 19th and early 20th centuries.

==History==

The development of the Saito Constitution was a direct consequence of the geopolitical pressures exerted by Western powers during the mid-19th century. For nearly three centuries, the policy of sakoku (isolation) had maintained the stability of the Honda Shogunate, but this isolation was shattered in 1853 by the arrival of Admiral Sir Arthur Catesby and his Iron Envoys. The superior technology demonstrated by the Mercian fleet, including telegraphs and steam engines, convinced the Sanese elite that the existing feudal structure was insufficient to protect national sovereignty.

The resulting internal friction led to the fall of the dual sovereignty system. In 1870, the final Shogun, Honda Yoshinobu, resigned his prerogatives to the Emperor in an event known as the ''Taisei Hōkan''. Although intended as a peaceful transfer, it triggered the Boshin War between Imperial loyalists and Shogunate hardliners. Following the Imperial victory at the Battle of Toba-Fushimi, the young Emperor Saito issued the Edict of Restoration on January 3, 1871, which formally abolished the Shogunate and commissioned the drafting of a modern constitution to unify the various domains.

The drafting process was led by Juichi Miyazaki, a statesman who had studied parliamentary systems in Mercia as part of the Sasedo Five. Miyazaki established a bureau to research Western legal frameworks, eventually traveling to Europe to consult with legal scholars in Briesland. He rejected more liberal models from the United States and France, viewing them as too permissive for a nation in the midst of a radical transition. Instead, he crafted a document that centralized authority in the throne while establishing the Diet of Okaiken to provide a controlled venue for public representation and budgetary oversight.

==Main Provisions==

The Saito Constitution was structured around the principle of Imperial sovereignty, as defined in Article 1, which stated that the Empire of Mitoyagi would be reigned over and governed by a line of Emperors unbroken for ages eternal. Article 3 declared the person of the Emperor to be sacred and inviolable, granting him supreme command over the Imperial Sanese Army and Navy. This prerogative allowed the military to operate independently of the civilian cabinet, a feature that would define Sanese politics for decades.

The document established a bicameral legislature known as the Diet of Okaiken. This consisted of the House of Officers, composed of the peerage and Imperial appointees, and the House of Commoners, whose members were chosen through limited franchise elections. While the Diet had the authority to approve the national budget and initiate legislation, the Emperor retained an absolute veto and the power to dissolve the lower house at any time.

Subjects of the Empire were granted several qualified rights, including freedom of speech, assembly, and religion, though these were strictly limited by the provision that they not be prejudicial to peace and order or antagonistic to the duties of subjects. The constitution also mandated duties such as the payment of taxes and compulsory military service. This legal framework remained unamended throughout its entire history, providing a stable if rigid foundation for the Imperial state until its eventual repeal.

